  "output": {
    "stories": [
      {
        "editor_spans": [
          {
            "body": "The full text changes something I wrote earlier. I said Westminster has no power to hand Scottish regions anything and could only shame Holyrood. That was too strong. The speech's one concrete offer is in an area Westminster really does control: DWP employment support. Some employability money and research-and-innovation funding are offered too, along with procurement rules. But look at where the DWP power would go. It would be 'shaped by Holyrood', not passed to Lanarkshire or Dundee. So the Prime Minister's first actual transfer adds to the centre he has just called one of Europe's most centralised. Whether any of it reaches the regions is left to Holyrood. That makes the offer more serious than I first allowed, and the rhetoric less consistent.",
            "cites": [
              352,
              305,
              306,
              299,
              153
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The money behind the speech is small next to its claims. There is £10 million for defence colleges, matched by Holyrood, and a £1 billion research budget spread across the whole UK. Much of the speech is spent on Ravenscraig and Leigh, where decisions taken far away and with no plan for what came next emptied whole communities. Yet the closures happening now get only short lines. On Grangemouth it says 'we will keep working hard'. On Aberdeen it says there will be 'more to say in due course'. The speech's test is whether anyone plans for what comes next. Aberdeen and Grangemouth are where that test is being taken now, and they get the least detail.",
            "cites": [
              175,
              213,
              183,
              108,
              84,
              92
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "There is also a gap in the diagnosis. The speech's sharpest statistic is a man in East Renfrewshire outliving a man in Glasgow by seven years, ten miles apart. Both places sit inside the same Glasgow City Region Deal. Handing power down to city regions does nothing about inequality within a city region. The Easterhouse approach the speech praises worked at the level of a single estate. If 'names not numbers' is the principle, the unit that matters is far smaller than a region. The National Economic Council meeting will show whether anyone has worked out how to build down to that level.",
            "cites": [
              117,
              271,
              274,
              315,
              316,
              318
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
